<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luis Vernet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Louis Vernet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luis Vernet was a merchant born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and in 1828 founded a settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a> on East Falkland. In 1829 the United Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> appointed him political and military commander of the islands. His seizure of American sealing ships in 1831 provoked a raid by the USS Lexington, and the British reasserted control of the islands in 1833.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luis-vernet.jpg" alt="Luis Vernet" width="302" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tochter von Luis Vernet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vernet_Luis_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vernet was born Louis Élie Vernet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> on 6 March 1791, the son of the tobacco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> merchant Jacques Vernet; his Huguenot ancestors, probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and then Hamburg. Fluent in German, French, English and Spanish, he called himself Ludwig, Louis, Lewis or Luis as the language required, and later claimed a French birthplace to win French support against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. At 14 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, where he joined the trading house of Krumbhaar, and he went on to trade with Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and Hamburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Vernet reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1818 aboard the frigate USS Congress, set up a trading company with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and on 17 August 1819 married María Saez Pérez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, with whom he had seven children. Through his friend Jorge Pacheco, who was owed money by the government, he took up a grant of 1823 to exploit the wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> of the Falklands. The first expedition of 1824 failed within days of landing, and a second, in 1826, struggled because the gauchos could not catch cattle in the boggy ground until the horses had been retrained.</p><p>In 1828 the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> granted him all of East Falkland, tax-free if he established a colony within three years. Each time before sailing he sought permission from the British consulate, and he kept up a friendly correspondence with the British consul, Woodbine Parish, telling him he had accepted his appointment as commander lest another governor harm his business. His deputy Matthew Brisbane tried to revive the settlement in 1833 but was murdered with other leading settlers by discontented gauchos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vernet never returned to the islands. He sold part of his holdings to the British merchant G. T. Whittington and gave maps and advice to Samuel Lafone, figures behind the British colony. A preservative treatment for leather credited to him paid for a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1852 to claim £14,295 in losses, and after some five years he was awarded £2,400. He died in San Isidro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, in 1871 and was buried in La Recoleta Cemetery. Argentines honour him as a national hero and also see him as a merchant who served his own interest, the US government accused him of piracy, and the British regard him as an entrepreneur who began the opening up of the islands' economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luis Vernet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1820s the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata appointed <b>Louis Vernet</b> as governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/falkland-islands">Falkland Islands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in San Isidro, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.55,10&amp;year=1843" aria-label="Hamburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Luis Vernet"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5506 5512h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5506 5512h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.55,10&amp;year=1843">What was here in 1843? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
